 "criteriaNotes": "Registered 22 Nov 2026 before any of these runs. The law (drainage.js grateCapture, the solver approach flows, the coupler taking the larger of it and the weir/orifice law) was written from HEC-22 and the solver code without running it on any gutter. Case a is the PV-SS13 street: the law has not seen it, but the failure it fixes was found there. Cases b are blind: a second cross slope, street width, roughness and range of slopes and rains. Cases c are the control and are expected to fail as PV-SS13 does (the weir law alone). Pass rule as PV-SS13: within 10 points of HEC-22 efficiency, kerb cell within 2 mm or 10 %. The fix is adopted only if every a and b row passes on cpu and gpu; the gutter depth rows must stay passed. Hand answers for b at registration: s05 r60: Q 12.6 l/s, T 1.41 m, V 0.42 m/s, E 0.903; s05 r150: Q 31.5 l/s, T 1.98 m, V 0.53 m/s, E 0.797; s15 r60: Q 12.6 l/s, T 1.14 m, V 0.64 m/s, E 0.916; s15 r150: Q 31.5 l/s, T 1.61 m, V 0.81 m/s, E 0.797; s30 r60: Q 12.6 l/s, T 1.00 m, V 0.83 m/s, E 0.937; s30 r150: Q 31.5 l/s, T 1.42 m, V 1.05 m/s, E 0.818",
 "conservation": "surface books (reported)",
 "resolution": "1 m cells: the gutter's triangular section is one cell's mean bed",
 "assumptions": [
